 to acquire as  an energetic and effective social  reformer he was recruited to be head  resident that is the director of the  University settling in New York City  his skills were immediately put to the  test in New York when he was appointed  head of the campaign committee whose  goal was to outlaw the employment of  children and mines factories and  sweatshops under his tireless leadership  New York passed the nation's first  effective child labor laws which became  models of the rest of the country  wellhow House was America's best-known  settlement house the university  settlement was older and more  prestigious having Roosevelt's and  Rockefellers among its patrons and  active participants it was here that  Robert met his future bride Caroline  despite good  despite the press's reaction to the  marriage when it was announced which  took place in 1903 he actually Adam the  match was actually approved by  Caroline's family and seems to have been  actively actively encouraged by  Caroline's mother who included the young  couple in a small group that she took  for a trip to the Grand Canyon and  private railroad car the newlyweds had  planned to spend their honeymoon in  England where Roberts had friends from a  previous visit  in 1899 however it turned into quite  remarkable an unconventional honeymoon  sometimes accompanied by car line and  sometimes alone hunter spent much of  this time in England meeting with  socialists and other leftist thinkers  and politicians he also visited  organisations that attempted to put  social its principles into practice the  proximity to England of other European  countries that promised to provide  similar or perhaps even more instructive  examples of social reform thinking and  action were too tempting for Robert to  pass up therefore their itinerary was  extended to include among other things a  visit to Leo toys Tolstoy at his country  estate in Russia visits to Gore Jewish  communities and the pale of settlement  between Russia and Poland and visits to  locations where they could see Germany's  comprehensive social welfare system in  action if you look at this picture  Caroline looks a little less sparkling  than she did  the pre-wedding photographs and maybe  that's because she actually was sick not  surprisingly got sick during this  traipse across Europe and they had to  spend three weeks for her to rest in  Warsaw before continuing their travels  this photograph was taken in Berlin  actually but they rested up in Warsaw  Chris Robert took the opportunity in  Warsaw to hunt down Polish partisans who  were promoting independence from Russia  were presently ruled that word at that  time rule Poland some of those folks  that he met during that time leader  became presidents of the Polish Republic  during the time that I became  independent in the early part of the  century at any rate it was an  interesting honeymoon trip upon the  return to New York Roberts book poverty  was published and it caused a sensation  opening Americans eyes for the first  time to the existence of widespread  poverty in the midst of unprecedented  wealth he estimated that at least 10  million and maybe as many as 20 million  people did not have a sufficient food  clothing or shelter to maintain a  minimal level of health and productivity  even more significantly he presented  convincing evidence that the great  majority of the poor were poor through  no fault of their own that most poverty  was socially caused and preventable by  appropriate government action this was  revolutionary thinking of the time and  made hunter a national authority on  social problems  HUL said poverty should be read by every  wealthy American and a New York Times  reviewer said it will have a million  readers  Hunter's advice was sought by the  nation's political and civic leaders  including President Theodore Roosevelt  who invited him to the house the White  House for consultation  it also propelled him into the ranks of  the social and cultural elite of the day  resulting in close friendships with Mark  Twain Jack London John Dewey Upton  Sinclair and others of like mind the  conclusions that flowed from poverty  that are that is the need for safe  workplaces sanitary housing workers  compensation widows pensions  unemployment insurance and a ban on  child labor fell far short of calling  for America's strengths for  transformation into a socialist society  in fact many of these social reforms  that hunter advocated soon became  central to the platforms of the newly  formed progressive party that Theodore  Roosevelt led as well as the progressive  wings of the Republican and Democratic  parties many of these reforms were  adopted actually adopted by individual  states and localities and served as  models for the national programs that  were later enacted under Franklin  Roosevelt's New Deal nevertheless Hunter  almost immediately joined the Socialist  Party because he along with an  astonishing cross-section of Americans  thought that it offered the best hope of  achieving the type of society they  believed this was a period the first two  decades of the 20th century the only  period in American history when  socialism was viewed as an acceptable  if not entirely respectable alternative  to the major political parties which had  become to be seen by hunter and others  as being a totally controlled  by big money they buy the melons the  Morgans the Rockefellers and the carnies  and at that time the people that Teddy  Roosevelt called  the malefactors of great wealth so they  joining the Socialist Party at that  particular time was not that unusual for  people even people of means its  membership included not only trade  unionists and intellectuals but also  religious leaders committed to the  social gospel a fair sprinkling of the  middle class and even a few millionaires  like hunter as he was by that time and  his brother-in-law Graham felt Stokes  could we take a look at that family  photograph of the Phelps Stokes family  this is the family that Robert hunter  married into sitting in the middle is  Anson Phelps Stokes one of the richest  men in America but also sitting in that  family photo or a number of socialists  Roberts not they're probably working to  finish up in poverty but sitting down  and the right-hand corner is Caroline a  member of the Socialist Party with  Robert jr. in her on her lap and behind  them second from the right is Caroline's  brother Graham Phelps Stokes also a  millionaire also a leader of the  Socialist Party of America next to  maryam is his wife Rose Pastore Stokes  Rose was an immigrant from Russia Jewish  immigrant from Russia who worked for  many years as a cigar factory worker in  Cleveland before moving to New York to  be a writer for the  New York Jewish Daily News and while  covering a story for the newspaper she  interviewed Graham Phelps Stokes they  were smitten with each other and got  married so here we have a number of  socialists she was a socialist before  she married Graham but here we have a  number of socialists in a very prominent  very wealthy American family at the  beginning of the last century hunter  rose quickly to a position of leadership  in the party being twice elected to the  National Executive Committee he also ran  as a socialist for the New York State  Assembly in 1907 and for believe it or  not governor of Connecticut in 1910  these were unsuccessful campaign  campaigns but others socialist  candidates were as successful in  elective office including mayor's quite  a number of state legislators and even a  couple of UST's congressmen while he was  active in the Socialist Party he  travelled to Europe as a delegate to  socialist congresses and while studying  the revolutionary movements that were  going on at that time and that brought  him in contact with such folks as Lenin  Trotsky Tolstoy as I'm noted and Maxim  Gorky Benito Mussolini  when Melissa Mussolini was an official  of the Italian Socialist Party George  Bernard Shaw and HTU else his accounts  of these encounters and his writings  make fascinating reading his friendships  and conflicts with such American  socialist leaders as the UD Eugene Debs  the  perennial much-beloved and perennial  socialist candidate for president Samuel  Gompers the longtime leader of the  American Federation of Labor Rose pastor  Stokes when we've seen here his  sister-in-law and Emma Goldman the  anarchist socialist and big bill Haywood  word leader of the International Workers  of the world the Wobblies as they were  called are also intriguing hunter  claimed that among his most important  contributions to the Socialist Party was  to persuade acquired and exhausted Deb's  to run for president for a third time in  1908 this blocked the threatened  candidacy of Hayward to lead the  socialist ticket a situation that in  view and the view of Hunter and hay  other party moderates would have been a  disaster for the socialist cause on  account of he words militancy struggles  between those like hunter and Deb's who  favored gradual societal change through  democratic processes and those like  Haywood who were willing to use any  means necessary including violence to  bring about the desired revolution began  to undermine hunters health and and  actually his faith in the socialist  cause when World War one came the  Socialist Party of America was virtually  destroyed by the conflicts over  America's entry into the war and the Red  Scare that followed Deb's and rose  pasture storks Stokes and many others  receive prison sentences for their  opposition to the war and just  disillusioned Robert hunter resigned  from the party and moved to California  for health health reasons he continued  to be to study the ways in which  societies might organize  selves to achieve fairness and  prosperity for all the citizens as  book-y we fail as Christians published  in 1919 reflected on his an ongoing  quest to find a way to 