ists of the time so there  is a question know her how did he he  maintained his religious belief how did  it play out in his work on behalf of the  poor and how did it relate to her there  are other religious leaders of the time  well he was very close to many of the  leaders of what was called the social  group and gospel movement that I  mentioned and these were people of  religion who believed very strongly that  the country the US was headed in the  wrong direction that that what was being  played out  on the national scene was the pursuit of  partner self-interest the pursuit of  greed the disregard for the poor among  us and that something needed to be done  about now lots of people involved in the  religious community were very active in  providing relief to the poor you know  making sure that people got food if they  were hungry provided shelter if they  could possibly provide it and clothing  and everything else that they were  capable of doing but the people in the  social gospel movement thought that it  was necessary to go beyond simply  offering handouts to the needy they saw  that that was insufficient and didn't  meet people's needs beyond the immediate  emergency what was needy was a reform of  the system so that it would be more fair  and would be in fact more in accord with  the teachings of all of the major  religions most of the folks active at  that time were you know you know people  of the Christian faith in the u.s. both  Protestants and Catholics but also there  was a significant movement in the Jewish  community to respond to these kinds of  means for example I mentioned the the a  settlement movement there were many  Jewish settlements that were active in  the major cities of the of the I was a  East and Northeast and Midwest who are  attempting to address these kind of  things so Hunter was a you know an ally  he didn't belong formally to the social  gospel movement but he was an ally  those religious leaders who said it  isn't enough to 